10032513,2024-09-05,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"I am writing to formally lodge a complaint against several credit reporting agencies for what I believe to be violations of federal laws concerning the reporting and disclosure of my credit information. My concerns are based on violations of the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ), the Privacy Act of 1974, the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act as well as 15 USC 1681 where the credit that is reporting should be 100 % accurate.

The credit bureaus are in violation because all three bureaus are not reporting the same information for one specific company.

1. Violation of 15 U.S.C. 1681a Exclusions : According to 15 U.S.C. 1681a ( 2 ) Exclusions ( A ) ( i ), credit reports should not contain information solely about transactions or experiences between the consumer and the reporting entity. I have observed that my credit report contains detailed transaction history, which appears to be in violation of this provision. This inclusion of transaction history is not only contrary to the FCRA but also impinges on the accuracy and fairness of my credit report.

2. Violation of the Privacy Act of 1974 ( 5 U.S.C. 552a ) : Under 5 U.S.C. 552a, no agency shall disclose any record contained in a system of records by any means of communication to any person or to another agency without prior written consent from the individual concerned. Specifically : Student Loans Reporting : My credit report contains information regarding my student loans that appears to be disclosed without my written consent. This disclosure is a potential violation of the Privacy Act unless it falls under specific exceptions such as routine uses or needs for the record by agency employees performing their duties. 
The law provides exceptions for disclosures, including : To officers or employees of the agency who need the record for their duties.

Required under Section 552 of this title.

Routine uses as defined under the Privacy Act.

Census or survey-related activities.

Statistical research or reporting records that are not individually identifiable.

3. Violation of 15 U.S.C. 6802 ( Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act ) : Under 15 U.S.C. 6802, financial institutions are prohibited from disclosing nonpublic personal information to third parties without proper authorization. I have noticed collections on my credit report that appear to have been shared with third parties without my consent, thereby violating this statutory requirement. The unauthorized sharing of my information is a serious concern and warrants immediate review.",Company has responded to the consumer and the CFPB and chooses not to provide a public response,"TRANSUNION INTERMEDIATE HOLDINGS, INC.",TX,75126,,Web,2024-09-05,Closed with explanation,Yes
